<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Byzacena</h1><p class="st-meta">303 CE – 439 CE (approx.) · capital Hadrumetum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The late Roman province of central Tunisia, an olive-growing land of prosperous towns governed from Hadrumetum.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 303 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> divided <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis</a> into smaller provinces as part of his reorganisation of the empire. The southern part of the old province, the region known as Byzacium, became Byzacena, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sousse">Hadrumetum</a> (Sousse), and was governed by a consularis within the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Byzacena was one of the richest farming regions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, covered with olive groves whose oil was shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and beyond. Towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-djem">Thysdrus</a>, with its great amphitheatre at El Djem, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufetula">Sufetula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaenae">Thaenae</a> flourished. The province had many bishoprics and was strongly affected by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatism">Donatist</a> controversy. Large imperial and private estates employed tenant farmers, the coloni.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> took control after capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in 439, and the region was part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vandal-kingdom">Vandal Kingdom</a> until Justinian's reconquest in 534 restored a province of Byzacena under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/praetorian-prefecture-of-africa">Praetorian Prefecture of Africa</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> kingdoms such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-capsus">Kingdom of Capsus</a> held its southern edges.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/byzacena.jpg" alt="Byzacena" width="320" height="255"><figcaption>Image: H. Kiepert · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Africa.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>303 CE – 439 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Hadrumetum</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Punic, Berber</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Byzacium, Provincia Byzacena</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=371&amp;focus=byzacena" aria-label="Byzacena on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4948 884 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Byzacena lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5268 1140L5328 1276M5268 1140L5304 942M5268 1140L5259 1106"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5328 1276h0M5304 942h0M5259 1106h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5268 1140h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5268 1140h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Hadrumetum. <a href="/atlas?year=371&amp;focus=byzacena">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
